  "content": {
    "id": "v2-d2748-p15-practice-slot-5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "For the curve $y=xe^{1-x}$ at $\\left(2,\\dfrac{2}{e}\\right)$, find the equation of the tangent and normal in general form.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Tangent: $x+ey-4=0$.\n\nNormal: $e^2x-ey+2-2e^2=0$.",
      "worked": "By the product and chain rules,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=e^{1-x}-xe^{1-x}\\\\&=(1-x)e^{1-x}\\\\m_{\\text{tangent}}&=(1-2)e^{1-2}\\\\m_{\\text{tangent}}&=-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}$$\nTangent:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{2}{e}&=-\\frac{1}{e}(x-2)\\\\ey-2&=-x+2\\\\x+ey-4&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe normal gradient is the negative reciprocal of the tangent gradient:\n$$m_{\\text{normal}}=e.$$\nNormal:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{2}{e}&=e(x-2)\\\\ey-2&=e^2(x-2)\\\\e^2x-ey+2-2e^2&=0.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
